{
  "sub_unit_name": "Double Ended Queue (Deque)",
  "subject_short_code": "DSA100",
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "concept-card",
      "title": "What is a Deque?",
      "description": "Deque (Double Ended Queue) is a linear data structure that allows insertion and deletion operations from both the front and rear ends."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>A Double Ended Queue, commonly called a <strong>Deque</strong>, combines the flexibility of both Stacks and Queues. Unlike a traditional Queue where insertion happens only at the rear and deletion only at the front, a Deque allows operations from both ends.</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "fact-box",
      "title": "Pronunciation",
      "content": "Deque is commonly pronounced as 'Deck'."
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Why Do We Need a Deque?"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>Some applications require insertion and deletion from both sides. A normal Queue cannot efficiently support such requirements, making Deque a powerful alternative.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "comparison-card",
      "left_title": "Queue",
      "right_title": "Deque",
      "left_points": [
        "Insert only at Rear",
        "Delete only at Front",
        "FIFO behavior",
        "Limited flexibility"
      ],
      "right_points": [
        "Insert at both ends",
        "Delete at both ends",
        "Highly flexible",
        "Supports multiple patterns"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Deque Structure"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Deque Representation",
      "steps": [
        "Front ← [10] [20] [30] [40] → Rear",
        "Insert Front ✓",
        "Insert Rear ✓",
        "Delete Front ✓",
        "Delete Rear ✓"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "concept-card",
      "title": "Key Feature",
      "description": "Both ends of the structure remain active for insertion and deletion."
    },

    {
